# Carrier Wound #35: The Verifier's Prompt Outgrew a Single argv Argument. execve E2BIG'd Before pi Ran.

Wound #34 made the *next* verifier failure legible — it threaded the agent's raw
output sample into the blocker so a non-arrival would stop reading as guesswork.
Then the next live drive arrived and the channel did exactly its job: it named a
failure that wasn't the agent's output at all. The agent never ran.

The deployed verifier went terminal again:

```
run-live-...-e4aa52ae
  → blocked / adapter_unavailable:
    "Configured verifier lane adapter failed: Verifier lane did not return a
     completed receipt for the requested lane: agent produced no parseable
     verdict (reason: no_parseable_output)."
```

Same surface as #33/#34 — `no_parseable_output` — but a different organ. The
planner (small prompt) reached `captured`. All 19 generated-workflow nodes
executed. Only the **meta-verifier** — the post-execution lane that reviews the
generated workflow's own proof (`createCloudflarePiVerifierLaneAdapter`) — came
back empty. The one lane whose prompt is the biggest: the verifier contract +
the full dynamic plan + every node's evidence (`JSON.stringify(…, null, 2)`) +
the capability receipts, all concatenated.

## The wound: a single argv argument has a hard ceiling, and the prompt blew past it

The lane command shipped the prompt to pi inline:

```
pi … -p "$(cat "$LANE_PROMPT_PATH")"
```

`$(cat …)` expands the **entire** prompt body into **one** argv argument. Linux
caps a single argv string at `MAX_ARG_STRLEN` = 32 pages = **128 KiB** — a
per-argument limit, distinct from the ~1–2 MiB total `ARG_MAX`. The verifier
prompt, fattened by all that inlined evidence, crossed 128 KiB. So `execve`
failed `E2BIG` ("Argument list too long") **before pi was ever exec'd**. The
shell wrote the error to the redirected stderr, stdout stayed empty (zero bytes),
the normalizer saw nothing to parse, and the lane committed a
`status:"failed"` / `no_parseable_output` receipt — byte-for-byte the same
terminal shape as a model that genuinely emitted nothing.

This is a resource-unavailable failure wearing an application-failure's clothes.
The argv buffer was the unavailable resource; the system tried the operation
anyway and got *partway* — far enough to commit a failed receipt and flow an
empty result downstream as if it were real.

> "If the system can determine in advance that it will fail at an operation,
> it's always better to fail fast. That way, the caller doesn't have to tie up
> any of its capacity waiting… There is a large class of 'resource unavailable'
> failures. … This is sort of the software equivalent of the cook's mise en
> place — gathering all the ingredients it will need to service the request
> before it begins. If any of the resources are not available, it can fail
> immediately, rather than getting partway through the work."
> — Michael Nygard, *Release It!*, §Fail Fast

The old form ignored mise en place: it shoved every ingredient onto a single
counter (one argv arg) that physically cannot hold them, and only discovered the
overflow mid-`execve`. The fix is to stop staging the prompt on that counter at
all.

## The fix: two halves — remove the ceiling, then make the next overflow legible

**Delivery half — `@file` reference (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`).**
The lane command now ships:

```
pi … -p "@$LANE_PROMPT_PATH"
```

Only the short *path* rides argv; pi reads the prompt body from disk via `@file`
message injection. The prompt can be 2 KiB or 2 MiB — argv no longer grows with
it, so `MAX_ARG_STRLEN` is structurally out of the loop. This is the carrier-class
fix: it removes the ceiling for **every** lane, not just the verifier, because
every lane delivers its prompt through the same builder.

The `--no-tools` worry is answered: under `--no-tools` there is no read tool to
*dereference* an attachment, so pi injects the `@file` content straight into the
message — semantically identical to the old inline `cat`, minus the argv ceiling.
A missing `@file` fails at message-assembly time ("File not found", pre-model),
so a bad path can't silently send an empty prompt.

**Legibility half — name the non-arrival (`cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.ts`).**
Even with the ceiling gone, *some* future overflow or crash will leave an empty
stdout again. So the blocker now distinguishes "process never produced output"
from "model emitted unparseable prose." The committed-failed receipt carries
`piExitStatus` and `stderrSample` (threaded through the normalizer →
receipt-builder → schema, riding the **committed receipt** road wound #34
established), and the verifier blocker appends `, pi exit: N` and `, stderr: …`
when present. An E2BIG non-arrival now reads `pi exit: 126 … stderr: …Argument
list too long` — Nygard's "report a system failure (resources not available)
differently than an application failure."

## Chaos tests that would have caught it (hostile doubles, not polite mocks)

Split across the two natural homes — one per half:

- **Delivery half** (`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts`). Slices the REAL
  delivery block out of `buildPiAgentLaneCommand()` and runs it under bash twice
  against a 2 MiB prompt and a fake `pi` on PATH. The shipped `@file` form: pi
  **runs** and the command reaches its post-pi step (`fileRefPiRan`,
  `fileRefReachedPostPi`). The pre-fix inline form — reconstructed by
  `replace('-p "@$LANE_PROMPT_PATH"', '-p "$(cat "$LANE_PROMPT_PATH")"')`, which
  **throws if the replace is a no-op** so a silent revert can't pass — `execve`
  E2BIGs: stderr matches `/Argument list too long/iu` and stdout is empty
  (`!inline.piRan`). The bug and the fix proven on the **real shell**, not a
  string-includes mock. The 2 MiB single arg overflows `MAX_ARG_STRLEN` on Linux
  and the ~1 MiB total `ARG_MAX` on macOS, so the class reproduces on either host.
- **Legibility half** (`cloudflare-agent-lane-adapters.test.ts`). A
  `driveE2big({piExitStatus, rawOutputSample, stderrSample})` helper builds a real
  `AgentLaneReceiptSchema`-parsed committed-failed verifier receipt and drives the
  REAL `createCloudflarePiVerifierLaneAdapter`, catching the thrown blocker.
  **A/B control, forced non-vacuous:** the E2BIG case (`piExitStatus: 126`,
  empty-stdout marker, `stderrSample:` the real "Argument list too long" tail)
  yields a blocker naming `pi exit: 126`, the empty marker, AND `Argument list
  too long`; the clean-exit control (`piExitStatus: 0`, `stderrSample: null`)
  OMITS both the `pi exit:` and `stderr:` clauses. Green only when exit + stderr
  actually survive schema-parse → adapter → blocker.

A polite mock asserting on a TS field would prove nothing about whether a 128 KiB
argv argument actually E2BIGs, nor whether the diagnostic survives the real schema
round-trip into the operator-facing string. These reproduce the exact transport:
real shell `execve`, real schema parse, real adapter building the real blocker.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · full suite **433 passed / 55 files**
(two new chaos tests over the prior 431).

## What this does NOT claim (skepticism held)

This fixes the verifier's **delivery** and makes the next non-arrival **legible**.
It does not, by itself, prove the dream run reaches `captured` — only a live
re-drive on the deployed fix does. The hypothesis is concrete and falsifiable: the
meta-verifier failed because its oversized prompt couldn't ride a single argv arg;
with `@file` delivery the same prompt reaches pi, pi emits a verdict, and the lane
normalizes to `completed`. If instead the verifier **still** fails empty, the
blocker now carries `pi exit` + `stderr` so the next diagnosis reads the actual
cause instead of guessing. Either outcome advances the carrier; neither is assumed.
One fresh deploy carries both halves, so the next live signal is unambiguous.

## The carrier discipline (fifteenth carrier wound)

#33 fixed *where the verdict lands*. #34 fixed *what you can see when it doesn't.*
#35 fixes *whether the agent could speak at all.* The class is not "the verifier
failed" — it's "**a delivery channel with an invisible size ceiling fails the
largest payload first, and fails it as a generic empty-output instead of a named
resource exhaustion.**" The biggest prompt (the meta-verifier's) is exactly the
one that overflows, so the failure looks like model silence precisely where the
payload is heaviest. The fix is Nygard's Fail Fast as a carrier invariant:
don't stage an unbounded payload on a bounded resource (argv) and discover the
overflow mid-operation — deliver by reference so the resource is never the
bottleneck, and when a resource *does* run out, report it as a system failure
(exit + stderr) distinct from an application failure (unparseable prose). An empty
stdout that could mean either is the worst of both: it reads as "the model said
nothing" while the truth is "the shell never let the model speak."
